<p class="lead">
  Marking a document DRAFT, CONFIDENTIAL, or with your company name before sharing it is one of the fastest ways to prevent a document from being mistaken for something it isn't — a work-in-progress treated as final, or an internal document treated as approved for external use.
</p>

<h2>Watermark it in your browser (no upload, free)</h2>
<p>
  The <a href="/tools/watermark-pdf/">Watermark PDF tool</a> on keptlocal stamps text diagonally across every page, entirely on your device.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Drop your PDF onto the tool.</li>
  <li>Type the watermark text — up to 40 characters. "CONFIDENTIAL" is the default.</li>
  <li>Pick a colour: gray, red, blue, or black.</li>
  <li>Pick a size: small, medium, or large.</li>
  <li>Adjust opacity with the slider — 10% (subtle) to 50% (bold).</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Apply &amp; download</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  The text is drawn at a 45° angle across the center of every page, scaled proportionally to the page size so it looks consistent whether the document is A4, Letter, or a custom size.
</p>

<h2>When a watermark is (and isn't) the right tool</h2>
<p>
  A visible text watermark is a communication device, not a security control — it makes a document's status obvious to anyone reading it, but it doesn't prevent someone from cropping, editing, or redistributing the file. Common, well-suited uses:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Marking drafts</strong> so a work-in-progress document is never mistaken for the final version if it's shared before review is complete.</li>
  <li><strong>Confidentiality notices</strong> on internal documents being shared with a limited audience — a visible reminder not to forward or publish.</li>
  <li><strong>Sample or preview marking</strong> — publishers and stock content providers watermark previews so the full, clean version stays valuable.</li>
  <li><strong>Attribution</strong> — stamping a company name or source across pages of a report being distributed publicly.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  If you actually need to prevent unauthorized copying or opening, that's a different problem — see keptlocal's <a href="/tools/protect-pdf/">Protect PDF</a> tool, which adds real AES-256 password encryption rather than a visible mark.
</p>

<h2>Choosing color and opacity</h2>
<p>
  Gray at 20–25% opacity is the most common choice for a professional document — visible without competing with the body text for attention. Red at higher opacity (35%+) reads as more urgent, which suits "DRAFT" or "DO NOT DISTRIBUTE" marks where the goal is to be impossible to miss. Blue and black sit between the two, useful when a document's existing color scheme makes gray or red clash visually.
</p>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your file</h2>
<p>
  The watermark is applied entirely in your browser using pdf-lib, a JavaScript PDF library — your file is never uploaded. Open DevTools → Network while watermarking and you'll see no outbound requests. This matters for anything marked "CONFIDENTIAL" in particular: sending a confidential document to a third-party server just to add a confidentiality stamp is a bit self-defeating.
</p>

